{"parentUuid":"61a609bb-3bbc-4371-b026-8df611157651","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"8929431c-6aa8-40a5-88cd-67ef17711815","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NBRzfvRLLes53sDwkvdAEd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPAGES 24\n2\t\n3\t===== PAGE 1 =====\n4\tBioHacks \n5\tMcMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario \n6\tBioHacks 2026 \n7\tHacker Package \n8\tNovember 7-8, 2026 \n9\t===== PAGE 2 =====\n10\tWelcome to\n11\tBioHacks\n12\t2026 \n13\tYou made it. For the next two days you and your\n14\tteam will take on one patient case, choose a track\n15\tand build something for him.\n16\tThis package holds the schedule, the prizes, how to\n17\tsubmit and the full case file. Read the case first.\n18\tThen go build. \n19\tDiscord \n20\tEvery announcement this weekend goes out on the BioHacks Discord.\n21\tdiscord.com/invite/mGZRP7vpj \n22\tInstagram \n23\tFollow \n24\t@_biohacks \n25\tand tag us in your posts from the weekend. \n26\tOrganizers \n27\tFind us at the check-in desk on the 2nd floor, outside the MCCD 2100 auditorium, or\n28\temail bioeng4youth@gmail.com. \n29\tBioHacks 2026   2 \n30\t===== PAGE 3 =====\n31\tSafety and help \n32\tMcMaster Security, 24 hours \n33\t905-522-4135 \n34\tFrom any campus phone, dial   88 EFRT , McMaster's Emergency First Response Team, is a\n35\tteam of student first responders on campus. Reach them\n36\tthrough Security at 905-522-4135 or 88. \n37\tEmergency   911 \n38\tAfter you call, tell the nearest organizer. \n39\tCode of conduct \n40\tBioHacks follows the MLH Code of\n41\tConduct. Harassment of any kind is not\n42\ttolerated. Report a concern to any\n43\torganizer, or to MLH at +1 343 453 4532\n44\tor incidents@mlh.io. \n45\tFire alarm \n46\tIf the alarm sounds, leave by the\n47\tnearest exit and wait outside until an\n48\torganizer says it is safe to return. \n49\tOvernight \n50\tHacking runs through the night. The\n51\tMCCD 2100 auditorium is open for\n52\tsleeping. Keep your laptop and\n53\tvaluables with you or with a\n54\tteammate. \n55\tHalal and vegetarian options are served at every meal. \n56\tBioHacks 2026   3 \n57\t===== PAGE 4 =====\n58\tSaturday, November 7 \n59\tCeremonies   Guest lectures   Workshops   Food   Judging\n60\tCheck-in, booths and deadline \n61\tSchedule \n62\t8 AM\n63\t9 AM\n64\t10 AM\n65\t11 AM\n66\t12 PM\n67\t1 PM\n68\t2 PM\n69\t3 PM\n70\t4 PM\n71\t5 PM\n72\t6 PM\n73\t7 PM\n74\t8 PM\n75\t9 PM\n76\t10 PM\n77\t11 PM\n78\t12 AM \n79\tDoors open and check-in \n80\t2nd floor, outside the MCCD 2100 auditorium \n81\tOpening ceremony \n82\tMCCD 2100 auditorium \n83\tTeam formation   Team registration closes at 12:00 PM \n84\tAnyone without a team is matched with one here. \n85\tLunch \n86\tArts Quad \n87\tCAD for Medical Devices, from Sketch to 3D Print \n88\tWorkshop, MCCD 3200 \n89\tHacking with GitHub Copilot   MLH workshop, MCCD 7150 \n90\tIntro to Google AI Studio   MLH workshop, MCCD 7150 \n91\tGuest lecture with Dr. Tandon \n92\tGUS Medical and Veterinary Schools, MCCD 3200 \n93\tGuest lecture with Dr. Rock \n94\tUHN Michener Institute of Education, MCCD 7140 \n95\tDinner   Arts Quad \n96\tTechTogether Meetup \n97\tMLH, MCCD 7140 \n98\tPitching workshop \n99\tMCCD 2100 auditorium \n100\tMidnight snack   MCCD 2100 auditorium \n101\tTeam form \n102\tSponsor booths \n103\t11:00 AM to 6:30 PM\n104\t2nd floor, outside the\n105\tauditorium \n106\tBioHacks 2026   4 \n107\t===== PAGE 5 =====\n108\tSunday, November 8 \n109\tCeremonies   Guest lectures   Workshops   Food   Judging\n110\tCheck-in, booths and deadline \n111\tSchedule \n112\t8 AM\n113\t9 AM\n114\t10 AM\n115\t11 AM\n116\t12 PM\n117\t1 PM\n118\t2 PM\n119\t3 PM\n120\t4 PM\n121\t5 PM\n122\t6 PM \n123\tBreakfast \n124\tMCCD 2100 auditorium \n125\tSubmissions due on Devpost at 11:30 AM\n126\tLunch \n127\tArts Quad \n128\tExpo judging \n129\tMCCD 7110 and 7120 \n130\tClosing ceremony \n131\tMCCD 2100 auditorium \n132\tEvent ends \n133\tBioHacks 2026   5 \n134\t===== PAGE 6 =====\n135\tSubmission and\n136\tjudging \n137\tProjects are due on Devpost\n138\tby 11:30 AM on Sunday\n139\t1   Team up.   Anyone without a team is matched with one during\n140\tteam formation at 11:00 AM on Saturday. Form a team of 2 to 4\n141\tand lock in one track on the team form by 12:00 PM on\n142\tSaturday. Each team competes in exactly that one track and\n143\tis judged only in it. \n144\t2   Build.   Workshops, guest lectures, the MLH hardware\n145\tlab and our 3D printers are open to every team. Ask\n146\tan organizer to print a part. \n147\t3   Submit.   One project per team on Devpost by\n148\t11:30 AM on Sunday at \n149\tbiohacksmcmaster.devpost.com .\n150\tExternal sponsor prizes are optional. A team\n151\tcan opt in to any of them on Devpost and win\n152\tthem alongside its track result.   Devpost \n153\t4   Present.   Expo judging runs from 1:30 to 3:00 PM on\n154\tSunday in MCCD 7110 and 7120. Judges come to\n155\tyour table. \n156\t5   Celebrate.   Winners are announced at the closing ceremony\n157\tat 4:30 PM in the MCCD 2100 auditorium. \n158\tBioHacks 2026   6 \n159\t===== PAGE 7 =====\n160\tAlex Chen \n161\tAlex is 47. He has taught senior biology at a high school on the Hamilton mountain for\n162\tnineteen years, writes everything on the whiteboard and walks the rows while students\n163\twork. He coached cross-country for a decade and ran half-marathons until the spring of\n164\t2025.\n165\tHe lives in Westdale with his partner Jordan, a medical radiation technologist on 7 am to 4\n166\tpm shifts, and their two children, Maya, 16, and Leo, 14. In June 2024 a student asked\n167\twhether his marker had run dry. His handwriting had started to fade out halfway across the\n168\tboard. The marker was fine. He waited a few months, then saw his family doctor. What\n169\tfollows is his file. \n170\tMountain Brow Family Health Team \n171\tHamilton, Ontario\n172\tReferral\n173\t14 October 2024\n174\tAlex Chen, 45, male\n175\tTo: Plastic and Hand Surgery Clinic \n176\tRe: Right hand weakness and deteriorating handwriting, four months \n177\tThank you for seeing Alex, a 45-year-old right-handed teacher who is otherwise well. Since June he has\n178\tnoticed that his handwriting fades across the page and that his right thumb and index finger tire quickly\n179\twhen writing on a whiteboard. He has started dropping his whiteboard marker and his coffee cup. No pain.\n180\tNo numbness or tingling. He reports some cramping in the right forearm at night.\n181\tOn examination the right thenar eminence looks a little flatter than the left. Grip is slightly weaker on the\n182\tright. Phalen's test was equivocal. Sensation to light touch was normal in all fingers. He works at a desk in the\n183\tevenings marking, and I suspect a carpal tunnel syndrome or a median neuropathy at the wrist.\n184\tI would be grateful for your assessment and for nerve conduction studies at the wrist. He has no significant\n185\tpast history, takes no medication, does not smoke and runs regularly. \n186\tDr. Priya Raman, MD, CCFP \n187\tFamily physician \n188\tPlastic and Hand Surgery Clinic \n189\tConsultation and follow-up. Dictated, not read.\n190\tConsultation 9 December 2024\n191\tFollow-up 21 April 2025\n192\tAlex Chen\n193\tCopy to: Dr. P. Raman \n194\tRe: Query right carpal tunnel syndrome \n195\tConsultation.   Six months of right hand weakness affecting fine tasks, no sensory symptoms. Nerve\n196\tconduction across the right wrist: median sensory and motor latencies within normal limits; no slowing\n197\tacross the carpal tunnel. Visible wasting of the right thenar muscles and the muscle between thumb and\n198\tindex finger. Grip 4 out of 5 on the right. Given the thenar wasting, his occupational demands and his wish to\n199\ttry something, I offered a right carpal tunnel release as a reasonable trial of treatment. He elected to\n200\tproceed after discussion of the risks and the uncertain benefit. Surgery 20 January 2025, uncomplicated. \n201\tFollow-up, three months after surgery.   Wound well healed. He reports no improvement. If anything the\n202\thand is weaker, and he now notices twitching under the skin of the forearm, which he describes as \"rippling\".\n203\tHe has also begun to mention his right foot catching on stairs. I have no further surgical options to offer.\n204\tReturned to the care of his family physician with a suggestion that a neurological opinion may be helpful if\n205\tsymptoms progress. \n206\tDr. Marcus Oyelaran, MD, FRCSC \n207\tPlastic and reconstructive surgery \n208\tBioHacks 2026   7 \n209\t===== PAGE 8 =====\n210\tWestdale Physiotherapy and Sports Clinic \n211\tDischarge summary\n212\t10 February 2026\n213\tAlex Chen\n214\t12 August 2025 to 5 February 2026, 18 sessions\n215\tTo: Dr. P. Raman \n216\tRe: Right foot drop, treated as peroneal weakness. Not improving. \n217\tAlex was referred in August 2025 for a right foot that catches on curbs and slaps on landing. He had\n218\tstopped running in May because the right leg \"felt heavy and would not do what I asked\". A lumbar\n219\tspine MRI in December 2025 was reported as unremarkable for his age, with no nerve root compression.\n220\tOver six months of strengthening, balance work and a trial of an off-the-shelf ankle brace, his right\n221\tankle dorsiflexion has gone from 4 out of 5 to 3 out of 5. Calf and thigh circumference on the right are\n222\teach about 2 cm smaller than the left. He now uses a cane outside the house. He reports two falls, both\n223\tin the evening, one on the stairs at home and one leaving the school at dusk. He has stopped cycling to\n224\twork and drives instead.\n225\tThree things concern me. The weakness is progressing despite treatment. His reflexes are brisk,\n226\tincluding at the right ankle, which is not what I would expect from a peripheral nerve problem. And he\n227\tmentions that his right hand has been weak for well over a year and that a wrist operation did not help\n228\tit. I do not think this is an ankle problem. I would strongly recommend a neurology referral, and I have\n229\ttold Alex so. \n230\tDaniela Ferreira, PT, MScPT \n231\tRegistered physiotherapist \n232\tMountain Brow Family Health Team \n233\tHamilton, Ontario\n234\tReferral, marked urgent\n235\t16 February 2026\n236\tAlex Chen, 46, male\n237\tTo: Neurology, outpatient clinic \n238\tRe: Progressive painless weakness, right arm and leg, twenty months \n239\tI would be grateful if you could see Alex soon. I first saw him in October 2024 with right hand weakness\n240\tthat I referred as a carpal tunnel syndrome. His wrist studies were normal, a carpal tunnel release in\n241\tJanuary 2025 did not help, and in the past year he has developed a right foot drop that physiotherapy\n242\thas not improved and that a lumbar MRI does not explain.\n243\tToday he reports muscle cramps, visible twitching in both forearms and lately the right thigh, and that\n244\the has lost about 6 kg without trying since the summer. His voice is unchanged as far as he is aware,\n245\talthough his partner says he is quieter in the evenings. There is no pain, no numbness, no change in\n246\tbladder or bowel function and no visual symptoms. On examination he has wasting of the right hand\n247\tand brisk reflexes in all four limbs. I am concerned about a progressive neurological condition and\n248\twould value your opinion. I am sorry this has taken as long as it has. \n249\tDr. Priya Raman, MD, CCFP \n250\tBioHacks 2026   8 \n251\t===== PAGE 9 =====\n252\tNeurology Outpatient Clinic \n253\tConsultation note. Neuromuscular service.\n254\tSeen 11 June 2026\n255\tReferral received 18 February 2026\n256\tAlex Chen, 47, male\n257\tReferring: Dr. P. Raman \n258\tReason for referral: progressive painless weakness, right arm and leg, now two years\n259\tHistory \n260\tRight-handed, 47. First symptom June 2024: handwriting fading on the whiteboard, fatigue in the right\n261\tthumb and index finger. Right carpal tunnel release January 2025 after normal wrist studies, no benefit.\n262\tRight foot drop from spring 2025, progressing through physiotherapy; lumbar MRI unremarkable.\n263\tCramps in both calves and the right forearm, worse at night. Fasciculations noticed in the forearms\n264\tsince April 2025, now also in the right thigh. Unintentional weight loss, 86 kg two years ago to 78 kg\n265\ttoday.\n266\tOn direct questioning: speech is clear in the morning but his partner and students notice slurring by late\n267\tafternoon, which he had put down to tiredness. Occasional coughing when drinking water quickly. He\n268\thas started sleeping on two pillows because he feels \"short of air\" lying flat. No diplopia, no sensory\n269\tsymptoms, no sphincter change. Mood is low but he denies hopelessness. He is still teaching full time\n270\tand still drives.\n271\tFamily history: no neurological disease in parents, siblings or grandparents. No dementia. Social: lives\n272\twith partner and two teenagers, non-smoker, two drinks a week. No toxin exposure. \n273\tExamination \n274\tGeneral.   Thin. Weight 78 kg. Normal cognition in conversation, but on a bedside screen he produced\n275\tonly nine words beginning with the same letter in one minute, with intact memory and orientation. \n276\tCranial nerves.   Eye movements full. Face symmetric. Tongue shows mild wasting with fasciculations at\n277\trest. Jaw jerk brisk. Palate elevates symmetrically. Speech mildly spastic in quality late in the\n278\tconsultation, intelligible. \n279\tUpper limbs.   Marked wasting of the right thenar eminence and first dorsal interosseous, with relative\n280\tsparing of the hypothenar muscles. Fasciculations in both forearms and the right deltoid. Tone\n281\tincreased on the right. Power: right grip 4/5, right finger abduction 3/5, right thumb abduction 3/5, left\n282\tupper limb 5/5. Reflexes brisk and symmetric, right more than left. Hoffmann sign present on the right. \n283\tLower limbs.   Right calf wasting. Fasciculations in the right quadriceps. Tone increased bilaterally. Power:\n284\tright ankle dorsiflexion 3/5, right hip flexion 4/5, left lower limb 5/5. Reflexes brisk, with three beats of\n285\tclonus at the right ankle. Right plantar response extensor, left flexor. \n286\tSensation.   Normal to light touch, pinprick, vibration and proprioception throughout.   Gait.   Right foot\n287\tdrop with a high-stepping gait and a cane. Unable to walk on heels on the right. Romberg negative. \n288\tInvestigations requested \n289\tNerve conduction studies and needle electromyography, four regions. MRI brain and cervical spine.\n290\tBloods as per neuromuscular panel. Pulmonary function tests upright and supine. Referral to speech-\n291\tlanguage pathology. Review in four weeks with results. \n292\tDr. Hannah Weiss-Okafor, MD, FRCPC \n293\tNeurologist, neuromuscular disease \n294\tBioHacks 2026   9 \n295\t===== PAGE 10 =====\n296\tElectromyography Laboratory \n297\tNerve conduction studies and needle EMG\n298\t18 June 2026\n299\tAlex Chen\n300\tRequested by: Dr. H. Weiss-Okafor \n301\tNerve conduction studies \n302\tNerve   Result   Reference \n303\tRight median motor, to thumb\n304\tmuscles \n305\tAmplitude 1.6 mV, latency and velocity\n306\tnormal\n307\tAmplitude above 4.0\n308\tmV \n309\tRight ulnar motor, to little-finger\n310\tmuscles \n311\tAmplitude 5.5 mV, velocity normal, no\n312\tconduction block\n313\tAmplitude above 6.0\n314\tmV \n315\tRight peroneal motor, to foot   Amplitude 0.9 mV, velocity normal   Amplitude above 2.0\n316\tmV \n317\tLeft median and ulnar motor   Amplitudes at lower limit of normal \n318\tSensory studies, median, ulnar,\n319\tsural, both sides \n320\tAll normal   Normal \n321\tNeedle electromyography \n322\tRegion   Muscles sampled   Findings \n323\tBulbar   Tongue   Fasciculation potentials, fibrillations, enlarged\n324\tmotor units \n325\tCervical   Right first dorsal interosseous,\n326\tright biceps, left deltoid\n327\tFibrillations and positive sharp waves,\n328\tfasciculations, large polyphasic motor units with\n329\treduced recruitment, right more than left \n330\tThoracic   Right T9 paraspinal   Fibrillations, fasciculations \n331\tLumbosacral   Right tibialis anterior, right vastus\n332\tlateralis, left gastrocnemius\n333\tActive denervation and chronic reinnervation in\n334\tall three; severely reduced recruitment in tibialis\n335\tanterior \n336\tSummary \n337\tWidespread active denervation and chronic reinnervation in four body regions, including clinically\n338\tunaffected muscles, with normal sensory conduction and no conduction block. There is no\n339\telectrophysiological evidence of a peripheral neuropathy, a disorder of the neuromuscular junction, or\n340\ta myopathy. \n341\tDr. Hannah Weiss-Okafor, MD, FRCPC \n342\tBioHacks 2026   10 \n343\t===== PAGE 11 =====\n344\tLaboratory Results \n345\tNeuromuscular panel, venous blood\n346\tCollected 12 June 2026\n347\tAlex Chen \n348\tTest   Result   Reference \n349\tCreatine kinase   470 U/L   Below 195 U/L \n350\tComplete blood count, electrolytes, renal and\n351\tliver function, glucose, calcium, thyroid,\n352\tvitamin B12 \n353\tAll within reference range \n354\tSerum protein electrophoresis and\n355\timmunofixation \n356\tNo paraprotein   None \n357\tHIV, syphilis, Lyme serology; anti-GM1\n358\tantibodies; heavy metals \n359\tNegative   Negative \n360\tSerum neurofilament light chain   88 pg/mL   Below 15 pg/mL at\n361\tage 47 \n362\tGenetic panel: C9orf72 repeat expansion, SOD1,\n363\tTARDBP, FUS and 28 further genes \n364\tNo pathogenic variant detected   Reported 30 July\n365\t2026 \n366\tPulmonary Function Laboratory \n367\tSpirometry, upright and supine\n368\t18 June 2026\n369\tAlex Chen \n370\tMeasure   Result   Reference \n371\tForced vital capacity, sitting   78 per cent of predicted   Above 80 per cent \n372\tForced vital capacity, lying flat   64 per cent of predicted   Within 10 per cent of\n373\tthe sitting value \n374\tSniff nasal inspiratory pressure   52 cm H 2 O   Above 70 cm H 2 O \n375\tComment: the fall in vital capacity on lying flat indicates diaphragm weakness. \n376\tBioHacks 2026   11 \n377\t===== PAGE 12 =====\n378\tDiagnostic Imaging \n379\tMRI brain and cervical spine, with and without contrast\n380\t16 June 2026\n381\tAlex Chen\n382\tRequested by: Dr. H. Weiss-Okafor \n383\tClinical information: progressive upper and lower limb weakness with brisk reflexes. Exclude cord\n384\tcompression. Query motor neuron disease. \n385\tA. Brain, axial FLAIR   at the level of\n386\tthe internal capsule. Symmetric\n387\thyperintensity along the posterior\n388\tlimb of the internal capsule on\n389\tboth sides (arrows), following the\n390\tcorticospinal tracts. \n391\tB. Brain, axial susceptibility-\n392\tweighted   near the vertex. A thin\n393\tband of low signal along the\n394\tprecentral gyrus on both sides\n395\t(arrows), sometimes called the\n396\tmotor band sign. \n397\tC. Cervical spine, sagittal T2. \n398\tNormal alignment. No disc\n399\tprotrusion, canal stenosis, cord\n400\tcompression or intrinsic cord\n401\tsignal change from the foramen\n402\tmagnum to T2. \n403\tImpression \n404\t- Bilateral, symmetric corticospinal tract signal change and a motor band sign. These findings are\n405\tsupportive of a degenerative process affecting the upper motor neurons in the appropriate clinical\n406\tcontext. They are not specific.\n407\t- No structural lesion of the brain or cervical cord to account for the patient's weakness. Specifically, no\n408\tcervical spondylotic myelopathy, no demyelinating lesion, no mass.\n409\t- No abnormal enhancement after contrast, so nothing to suggest tumour, infection or inflammation. \n410\tDr. Thanh Nguyen, MD, FRCPC \n411\tNeuroradiology \n412\tBioHacks 2026   12 \n413\t===== PAGE 13 =====\n414\tNeurology Outpatient Clinic \n415\tLetter to referring physician. Copy given to the patient at his request.\n416\t14 July 2026\n417\tAlex Chen, 47, male\n418\tTo: Dr. P. Raman \n419\tDiagnosis: amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), limb onset, with early bulbar and respiratory\n420\tinvolvement \n421\tDear Dr. Raman,\n422\tI saw Alex and his partner Jordan today to go through his results, and I am sorry to say that the\n423\tdiagnosis is ALS. I spent the appointment with them and the clinic social worker, and they will return in\n424\ttwo weeks to talk further. He asked that this letter be written in plain language and that he be given a\n425\tcopy, which I have done. \n426\tHow the diagnosis was made.   ALS is diagnosed on the clinical picture, not on any single test. Alex has\n427\tprogressive weakness with signs of both upper motor neuron damage (brisk reflexes, clonus, a\n428\tHoffmann sign, an upgoing plantar, spastic speech) and lower motor neuron damage (wasting,\n429\tfasciculations, low motor amplitudes, denervation on EMG) in the same regions, now spread across all\n430\tfour: bulbar, cervical, thoracic and lumbosacral. Sensation is normal. The MRI excludes the structural\n431\tconditions that can mimic this, the bloods exclude the treatable ones, and the EMG shows the process\n432\tis active and widespread. He meets the Gold Coast criteria without ambiguity. In retrospect he probably\n433\tmet them a year ago. \n434\tWhat ALS is.   A progressive loss of the motor neurons that drive voluntary muscle, in the brain and the\n435\tspinal cord. The muscle itself is healthy; it wastes because the nerve supply dies. Sensation, eye\n436\tmovements, bladder and bowel are spared. Thinking is usually spared, though about half of patients\n437\tdevelop some change in word-finding or executive function, and Alex's reduced verbal fluency is of that\n438\tkind. The cause in his case, as in about nine of ten cases, is unknown. \n439\tTreatment.   There is no cure. Two drugs have been shown to slow progression modestly: riluzole, which I\n440\thave started today, and edaravone, which we will discuss. A third drug, tofersen, is the first treatment to\n441\tact on a cause of ALS. It is an antisense oligonucleotide that silences a faulty SOD1 gene, and because it\n442\tcannot cross the blood-brain barrier it is given by lumbar puncture every month. Health Canada\n443\tauthorised it with conditions in February 2025. It applies to the roughly two per cent of patients who\n444\tcarry a SOD1 mutation. Alex's genetic panel was negative, so, like most people with this disease, he has\n445\tno treatment directed at its cause. Beyond drugs, what has been shown to lengthen life is care at a\n446\tmultidisciplinary clinic, keeping his weight up, and breathing support at night once it is needed. \n447\tWhat to expect.   His progression over two years has been steady rather than rapid, which is in his\n448\tfavour. The pattern will continue: the right hand and leg will weaken further and the left side will follow;\n449\tspeech, swallowing and breathing have begun to be involved and will progress. I have been honest with\n450\tthem that the median survival from symptom onset is two to four years, that the range is wide, and that\n451\tthe decisions ahead (a feeding tube, ventilation at night, how he wants to communicate when speech\n452\tis difficult) are better made early. I have referred him to the regional ALS clinic, which will see him every\n453\tthree months, and to ALS Canada for equipment and support.\n454\tHe intends to keep teaching this year. I see no reason he should not. \n455\tDr. Hannah Weiss-Okafor, MD, FRCPC \n456\tNeurologist, neuromuscular disease \n457\tBioHacks 2026   13 \n458\t===== PAGE 14 =====\n459\tRegional ALS Clinic \n460\tMultidisciplinary summary. First visit.\n461\t22 September 2026\n462\tAlex Chen, 47\n463\t27 months from first symptom, 10 weeks from diagnosis \n464\tMedication   Riluzole 50 mg twice daily since July. Tolerating. Edaravone declined for now.\n465\tLiving situation   Two-storey house, bedroom and only full bathroom upstairs, fourteen stairs with a rail\n466\ton the left. Partner works 7 am to 4 pm; Alex is alone at home on his non-teaching\n467\tdays and from the end of school until 4:30 pm. Children are at school until 3 pm.\n468\tWork   Full-time teaching, four classes a day. School has agreed to a stool and a microphone\n469\ton request; he has not asked for either. \n470\tNeurology \n471\tALSFRS-R 32 of 48 (bulbar 9, fine motor 6, gross motor 7, breathing 10). Decline of about 0.6 points per\n472\tmonth since onset, roughly linear. Right hand now 3/5 grip, cannot sustain a pinch for more than a few\n473\tseconds. Left hand weaker than in June: grip 4/5, early first dorsal interosseous wasting. Cognitive\n474\tscreen (ECAS) 101 of 136, with verbal fluency and executive scores below cut-off and memory, language\n475\tand visuospatial scores normal. \n476\tSpeech-language pathology \n477\tSpeech is intelligible but effortful by late afternoon. Reading passage: 165 words per minute at 9 am, 118\n478\tat 4 pm; sentence intelligibility 98 per cent in the morning, 91 per cent in the afternoon. He reports that\n479\tstudents at the back of the room have started asking him to repeat himself after lunch and that he has\n480\tstopped doing the end-of-day recap. Voice banking discussed; he has not started and is reluctant (\"it\n481\tfeels like giving up\"). Timed water swallow: cough on thin liquid; solids and thickened liquids unaffected.\n482\tHe has started avoiding eating in the staff room. \n483\tDietitian \n484\tWeight 77.5 kg, from 86 kg. Meals take 35 to 40 minutes; he tires before finishing. Right-handed cutlery\n485\tno longer practical; he has been eating with a spoon in the left hand. Calorie supplement started.\n486\tFeeding tube discussed as a future decision while breathing capacity is still above 50 per cent. He says\n487\tSunday cooking for the family is the thing he least wants to give up. \n488\tOccupational therapy \n489\tHandwriting now illegible after about ten minutes. Marks by typing with the left hand and two right\n490\tfingers; phone use is one-handed and slow. Buttons, zips and shoelaces take help in the morning. Drives\n491\tan automatic with the left foot off the pedals, which he has not told his insurer. Stairs are the main\n492\thazard: both falls were after 6 pm on the stairs or at dusk. Advised against carrying anything on the\n493\tstairs. Home assessment booked. He has declined a stairlift \"for now\". \n494\tPhysiotherapy and respiratory \n495\t10-metre walk with cane 14.2 seconds. Ankle-foot orthosis fitted on the right, which he wears to school\n496\tbut not at home. Forced vital capacity 76 per cent sitting, 61 per cent supine. Sleeps on two pillows,\n497\twakes once or twice with a dry mouth. Non-invasive ventilation discussed; not yet indicated. Cough\n498\tpeak flow 310 L/min. \n499\tSocial work \n500\tHas disclosed to the school and to the children. Finances adequate while he works; partner's shifts\n501\tcannot easily change. Wants to be \"reachable and useful\" while alone in the afternoons. Would like to\n502\tget to his daughter's graduation in two years without a wheelchair. \n503\tBioHacks 2026   14 \n504\t===== PAGE 15 =====\n505\tMeasurements \n506\tMeasure   Alex   Typical\n507\tHand \n508\tGrip strength, dynamometer   Right 14 kg, left 32 kg   About 48 kg \n509\tKey pinch   Right 3 kg, left 8 kg   About 11 kg \n510\tNine-hole peg test   Right 44 s, left 23 s   About 20 s \n511\tSpeech and swallowing \n512\tSpeaking rate, same passage, 9 am and 4\n513\tpm \n514\t165 and 118 words per minute   150 to 190 \n515\tSentence intelligibility, 9 am and 4 pm   98 and 91 per cent   Above 97 per cent \n516\tTimed water swallow, 100 mL   Cough on thin liquid   No cough \n517\tBreathing \n518\tForced vital capacity, sitting and lying flat   76 and 61 per cent of\n519\tpredicted\n520\tAbove 80; within 10 per\n521\tcent of sitting \n522\tWalking \n523\t10-metre walk with cane   14.2 s   Under 8 s \n524\tFalls in the past three months   Two, both after 6 pm   None \n525\tProgression \n526\tALSFRS-R at 0, 6, 12, 18, 24 and 27 months   48, 43, 39, 36, 33, 32   48 \n527\tSerum neurofilament light chain   88 pg/mL   Below 15 pg/mL at his age \n528\tWeight   77.5 kg; 86 kg two years ago \n529\tBioHacks 2026   15 \n530\t===== PAGE 16 =====\n531\tFrom Alex \n532\tYou have just read two years of my life in about twenty minutes.\n533\tI am still me. I still get up at six and I still argue about the Leafs. This thing\n534\thas taken my right hand and some of my voice, and it is going to take\n535\tmore. The people around me are still here. My wife still laughs at my\n536\tjokes. My kids still climb on me. My students still email me at midnight.\n537\tI want to be in a classroom in June. I want to be the one who cooks on\n538\tSunday, even if it takes me all afternoon. I want my kids to remember me\n539\tas their dad and not as a patient.\n540\tWhatever you build this weekend, I hope it comes from a place of caring\n541\tabout someone like me. If it helps one person hold on to one of those\n542\tordinary things a little longer, you will have done something that matters\n543\tto a whole family. Thank you for spending your weekend on us. \n544\tAlex \n545\tBioHacks 2026   16 \n546\t===== PAGE 17 =====\n547\tThe tracks \n548\tChoose one track to focus on. \n549\t1. Detection and Diagnostics \n550\tAlex waited two years for a diagnosis, through four clinicians and an\n551\toperation that did nothing.\n552\tIn Canada the wait is typically 15 to 27 months depending on the province.\n553\tSince diagnosis his disease is followed with a questionnaire every three\n554\tmonths.\n555\tBuild something that detects the disease earlier or predicts how it will\n556\tprogress. Use the datasets on the next page or find your own. \n557\t2. Therapeutics and Delivery \n558\tThe one drug that acts on a cause of ALS applies to about two in a\n559\thundred patients. Alex is not one of them.\n560\tThe two other approved drugs add months. More than fifty late-stage trials\n561\thave failed since riluzole was approved in 1995.\n562\tGetting anything into a spinal motor neuron is itself an unsolved problem.\n563\tPropose a therapy, or a way of delivering one, that could slow the disease\n564\tor protect the neurons that remain. Say what it would mean for Alex now\n565\tand for someone where he was in 2024. \n566\t3. Wearable Devices and Quality of Life \n567\tHe is strong in the morning and weak by the afternoon. He teaches in a\n568\tclassroom where he needs to be heard, he is alone at home until 4:30, and\n569\the has fallen on the stairs.\n570\tHis right hand has a few seconds of pinch left. His left is starting to go.\n571\tMost assistive devices end up in a drawer because nobody asked the\n572\tperson what they wanted.\n573\tDesign a device or tool that lets Alex keep one thing that matters to him as\n574\this abilities change. The MLH hardware lab is open all weekend if you want\n575\tto build. \n576\tBioHacks 2026   17 \n577\t===== PAGE 18 =====\n578\tData \n579\tDataset   What it is   Access \n580\tALS Focus, Harvard\n581\tDataverse \n582\tSurvey data from the ALS Association: self-reported\n583\tALSFRS-R and vital capacity over time (892 records),\n584\tdemographics for 1,545 people with ALS and\n585\tcaregivers, health status and telehealth responses,\n586\twith a data dictionary.\n587\tOpen download after\n588\taccepting the data use\n589\tterms. No account. \n590\tALS progression data,\n591\tEfron and Hastie \n592\t1,822 people with ALS from the 2012 DREAM challenge,\n593\tdrawn from clinical trial records: demographics, onset\n594\tsite, symptoms, ALSFRS items, vital capacity, vitals and\n595\tweight summarised per patient, with the rate of\n596\tALSFRS decline (dFRS) as the target. 371 columns.\n597\tDocumented in the CASIdata R package.\n598\tOpen download, one text\n599\tfile. No account. \n600\tGait in\n601\tNeurodegenerative\n602\tDisease, PhysioNet \n603\tFoot-force and stride-interval recordings from 13\n604\tpeople with ALS, 15 with Parkinson's, 20 with\n605\tHuntington's and 16 controls.\n606\tOpen download, no\n607\taccount. \n608\tVOC-ALS, Synapse   1,224 recordings of sustained vowels and syllable\n609\trepetition from 102 people with ALS and 51 matched\n610\tcontrols, labelled with ALSFRS-R speech score and\n611\tdysarthria severity. Paper: Scientific Data 2024.\n612\tFree Synapse account\n613\t(registered user).\n614\tSynapse terms of use. \n615\tbigP3BCI, PhysioNet   P300 speller EEG from 326 participant sessions across\n616\t20 studies, including people with ALS with ALSFRS-R in\n617\tthe file headers. 10 GB zipped; single studies can be\n618\tpulled separately.\n619\tOpen download. CC BY\n620\t4.0. \n621\tEEGET-ALS, Figshare   32-channel EEG and eye tracking from six people with\n622\tALS using an eye-gaze speller at home over several\n623\tmonths, and 170 controls. 1.6 GB. Paper: Scientific Data\n624\t2024.\n625\tOpen download, no\n626\taccount. CC0. \n627\tSeattle ALS Patient\n628\tProfile Database \n629\tInterviews every three months for 18 months with 143\n630\tpeople with ALS and 123 partners: coping, support,\n631\tdevices, psychological health, outcomes. Mendeley\n632\tData.\n633\tOpen download. CC BY-\n634\tNC 3.0. \n635\tBioHacks 2026   18 \n636\t===== PAGE 19 =====\n637\tReferences \n638\t1. ALS Society of Canada. ReferALS early referral tool for physicians and patients.\n639\tals.ca/resource/referals-early-referral-tool\n640\t2. Shefner JM, Al-Chalabi A, Baker MR, et al. A proposal for new diagnostic criteria for ALS (the Gold\n641\tCoast criteria). Clin Neurophysiol. 2020;131(8):1975-1978.\n642\t3. Richards D, Morren JA, Pioro EP. Time to diagnosis and factors affecting diagnostic delay in\n643\tamyotrophic lateral sclerosis. In: Araki T, ed. Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis. Exon Publications; 2021.\n644\t4. Hodgkinson VL, et al. Provincial differences in the diagnosis and care of amyotrophic lateral\n645\tsclerosis. Can J Neurol Sci. 2018;45(6):652-659.\n646\t5. Westeneng HJ, et al. Prognosis for patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: development and\n647\tvalidation of a personalised prediction model. Lancet Neurol. 2018;17(5):423-433.\n648\t6. Health Canada. Summary Basis of Decision, Qalsody (tofersen). Notice of Compliance with\n649\tconditions, 28 February 2025.\n650\t7. Phillips B, Zhao H. Predictors of assistive technology abandonment. Assist Technol. 1993;5(1):36-45.\n651\t8. Ontario Ministry of Health. Assistive Devices Program. ontario.ca/page/assistive-devices-program \n652\tA note on the images \n653\tThe MRI images on page 12 are reproduced from open-access teaching material. They illustrate the\n654\tfindings described in the report and are not from one patient. Alex, his family and every clinician and\n655\tpractice in this file are fictional. \n656\tBioHacks 2026   19 \n657\t===== PAGE 20 =====\n658\tJudging rubric \n659\tDetection and Diagnostics \n660\tCriterion   Level 4   Level 3   Level 2   Level 1\n661\tClinical\n662\tgrounding \n663\t20 \n664\t20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \n665\tDiagnostic\n666\tvalue \n667\t30 \n668\t30 points   22 points   15 points   8 points \n669\tEvidence and\n670\ttesting \n671\t20 \n672\t20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \n673\tFeasibility \n674\t15 \n675\t15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \n676\tCommunication \n677\t15 \n678\t15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \n679\tClear connection\n680\tto the patient's\n681\tfindings, abilities\n682\tand priorities\n683\tDesign choices\n684\tfollow from the\n685\tcase\n686\tGood connection\n687\tto the case\n688\tSome design\n689\tchoices not tied\n690\tto it\n691\tLoose connection\n692\tto the case\n693\tCould apply to\n694\tany patient with\n695\tthis condition\n696\tLittle or no\n697\tconnection to the\n698\tcase\n699\tClear explanation\n700\tof what is\n701\tdetected and why\n702\tit matters\n703\tClear advantage\n704\tover current\n705\tpractice\n706\tGood explanation\n707\tof what is\n708\tdetected\n709\tAdvantage over\n710\tcurrent practice\n711\tstated with minor\n712\tgaps\n713\tAdequate\n714\texplanation of\n715\twhat is detected\n716\tAdvantage over\n717\tcurrent practice\n718\tunclear\n719\tUnclear what is\n720\tdetected or why it\n721\tmatters\n722\tStrong reasoning\n723\tfor why it works,\n724\tsupported by\n725\tdata,\n726\tmeasurements or\n727\ta prototype\n728\tClear plan for how\n729\tthe method would\n730\tbe tested and\n731\taccuracy\n732\tmeasured\n733\tGood reasoning\n734\twith some\n735\tsupporting\n736\tevidence\n737\tGood testing plan\n738\twith minor gaps\n739\tReasoning\n740\tpresent but little\n741\tevidence\n742\tTesting\n743\tmentioned but\n744\tnot developed\n745\tNo clear\n746\treasoning or\n747\tevidence\n748\tNo testing\n749\tconsidered\n750\tClear and realistic\n751\tpath to use, with\n752\tcost, access and\n753\tregulation\n754\tconsidered\n755\tGood\n756\tconsideration of\n757\tpractical barriers\n758\twith minor gaps\n759\tPractical barriers\n760\tacknowledged\n761\tbut not addressed\n762\tPractical barriers\n763\tmissing or\n764\tignored\n765\tClear, organised\n766\tand concise\n767\tQuestions\n768\tanswered well\n769\tClear overall with\n770\tminor issues\n771\tMost questions\n772\tanswered\n773\tHard to follow in\n774\tplaces\n775\tQuestions partly\n776\tanswered\n777\tUnclear or\n778\tdisorganised\n779\tQuestions not\n780\tanswered \n781\tJudges score each project out of 100 with this rubric, then name the best, second\n782\tbest and third best project they saw in the track. The rankings from all judges\n783\tdetermine which two teams win in each track. \n784\tBioHacks 2026   20 \n785\t===== PAGE 21 =====\n786\tJudging rubric \n787\tTherapeutics and Delivery \n788\tCriterion   Level 4   Level 3   Level 2   Level 1\n789\tClinical\n790\tgrounding \n791\t20 \n792\t20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \n793\tMechanism\n794\tand rationale \n795\t30 \n796\t30 points   22 points   15 points   8 points \n797\tReaching the\n798\tpatient \n799\t20 \n800\t20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \n801\tFeasibility \n802\t15 \n803\t15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \n804\tCommunication \n805\t15 \n806\t15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \n807\tClear connection\n808\tto the patient's\n809\tfindings, abilities\n810\tand priorities\n811\tDesign choices\n812\tfollow from the\n813\tcase\n814\tGood connection\n815\tto the case\n816\tSome design\n817\tchoices not tied\n818\tto it\n819\tLoose connection\n820\tto the case\n821\tCould apply to\n822\tany patient with\n823\tthis condition\n824\tLittle or no\n825\tconnection to the\n826\tcase\n827\tClear and well\n828\tsupported\n829\texplanation of\n830\thow the\n831\ttreatment acts on\n832\tthe condition\n833\tScientifically\n834\tsound\n835\tGood explanation\n836\tof the mechanism\n837\twith minor gaps\n838\tor light support\n839\tMechanism\n840\tstated but weakly\n841\texplained or\n842\tsupported\n843\tMechanism\n844\tunclear or\n845\tmissing\n846\tClear explanation\n847\tof how the\n848\ttreatment\n849\treaches where it\n850\tacts\n851\tMain risks\n852\tidentified and\n853\taddressed\n854\tGood explanation\n855\tof delivery\n856\tRisks identified\n857\twith minor gaps\n858\tDelivery or risks\n859\tmentioned but\n860\tnot developed\n861\tDelivery and risks\n862\tnot considered\n863\tClear and realistic\n864\tpath to use, with\n865\tcost, access and\n866\tregulation\n867\tconsidered\n868\tGood\n869\tconsideration of\n870\tpractical barriers\n871\twith minor gaps\n872\tPractical barriers\n873\tacknowledged\n874\tbut not addressed\n875\tPractical barriers\n876\tmissing or\n877\tignored\n878\tClear, organised\n879\tand concise\n880\tQuestions\n881\tanswered well\n882\tClear overall with\n883\tminor issues\n884\tMost questions\n885\tanswered\n886\tHard to follow in\n887\tplaces\n888\tQuestions partly\n889\tanswered\n890\tUnclear or\n891\tdisorganised\n892\tQuestions not\n893\tanswered \n894\tJudges score each project out of 100 with this rubric, then name the best, second\n895\tbest and third best project they saw in the track. The rankings from all judges\n896\tdetermine which two teams win in each track. \n897\tBioHacks 2026   21 \n898\t===== PAGE 22 =====\n899\tJudging rubric \n900\tWearable Devices and Quality of Life \n901\tCriterion   Level 4   Level 3   Level 2   Level 1\n902\tClinical\n903\tgrounding \n904\t20 \n905\t20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \n906\tDesign and\n907\tfunction \n908\t30 \n909\t30 points   22 points   15 points   8 points \n910\tUsability in\n911\tdaily life \n912\t20 \n913\t20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \n914\tFeasibility \n915\t15 \n916\t15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \n917\tCommunication \n918\t15 \n919\t15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \n920\tClear connection\n921\tto the patient's\n922\tfindings, abilities\n923\tand priorities\n924\tDesign choices\n925\tfollow from the\n926\tcase\n927\tGood connection\n928\tto the case\n929\tSome design\n930\tchoices not tied\n931\tto it\n932\tLoose connection\n933\tto the case\n934\tCould apply to\n935\tany patient with\n936\tthis condition\n937\tLittle or no\n938\tconnection to the\n939\tcase\n940\tClear and\n941\tcomplete design\n942\tthat does what it\n943\tclaims\n944\tSupported by a\n945\tprototype, model\n946\tor demonstration\n947\tGood design with\n948\tminor gaps\n949\tLimited\n950\tdemonstration\n951\tPartial design or\n952\tunclear function\n953\tUnclear what the\n954\tdevice does\n955\tComfortable and\n956\tlow effort for the\n957\tpatient\n958\tAdapts as the\n959\tcondition\n960\tprogresses\n961\tUsable day to day\n962\tAdapts to some\n963\tprogression of the\n964\tcondition\n965\tUsable only in\n966\tideal conditions\n967\tImpractical for\n968\tdaily use\n969\tClear and realistic\n970\tpath to use, with\n971\tcost, access and\n972\tregulation\n973\tconsidered\n974\tGood\n975\tconsideration of\n976\tpractical barriers\n977\twith minor gaps\n978\tPractical barriers\n979\tacknowledged\n980\tbut not addressed\n981\tPractical barriers\n982\tmissing or\n983\tignored\n984\tClear, organised\n985\tand concise\n986\tQuestions\n987\tanswered well\n988\tClear overall with\n989\tminor issues\n990\tMost questions\n991\tanswered\n992\tHard to follow in\n993\tplaces\n994\tQuestions partly\n995\tanswered\n996\tUnclear or\n997\tdisorganised\n998\tQuestions not\n999\tanswered \n1000\tJudges score each project out of 100 with this rubric, then name the best, second\n1001\tbest and third best project they saw in the track. The rankings from all judges\n1002\tdetermine which two teams win in each track. \n1003\tBioHacks 2026   22 \n1004\t===== PAGE 23 =====\n1005\tPrizes \n1006\tFirst place \n1007\t$1,000 in prizes for the top team overall \n1008\tEach track has two winning teams. Track winners receive cash\n1009\tprizes, with amounts announced on Sunday. \n1010\tExternal sponsor prizes \n1011\tThese prizes sit outside the three tracks. A team can win any of them alongside its track\n1012\tresult. Opt in on Devpost. \n1013\tBest 3D Print \n1014\tFilament \n1015\tBest Use of\n1016\tElevenLabs \n1017\tWireless\n1018\tearbuds \n1019\tBest Use of Gemini\n1020\tAPI \n1021\tMLH swag kits \n1022\tBest Use of Entire \n1023\tMini portable\n1024\tprojector \n1025\tBest Use of Solana \n1026\tUnruggable\n1027\thardware\n1028\twallets \n1029\tBest Use of Tiger\n1030\tData \n1031\tStream Deck\n1032\tMini \n1033\tBest Use of Vultr \n1034\tAssorted prizes \n1035\tBest Domain Name\n1036\tfrom GoDaddy\n1037\tRegistry \n1038\tDigital gift card \n1039\tBest vlog \n1040\t$100 Amazon gift\n1041\tcard \n1042\tVlog your weekend at BioHacks, post it on\n1043\tInstagram and tag @_biohacks, then drop\n1044\tthe link in the vlogs channel on the Discord.\n1045\tThe best vlog wins. \n1046\tWorkshop raffle \n1047\t1 year of NordVPN, NordPass and\n1048\tIncogni, plus Saily eSIM data \n1049\tAttend three of the four workshops and both\n1050\tguest lectures to enter. Six winners are drawn\n1051\tat the closing ceremony. \n1052\tBioHacks 2026   23 \n1053\t===== PAGE 24 =====\n1054\tHosts, partners and sponsors \n1055\tHosts \n1056\tBioHacks   iBioMed Society \n1057\tPartner\n1058\tHeadline sponsors \n1059\tSponsors \n1060\tMLH partners \n1061\tBioHacks 2026   24 "}]},"uuid":"a4806ce1-e177-4e00-852c-aae485c1dbf3","timestamp":"2026-11-08T06:04:12.148Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\jerem\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\2ea77955cf64b3d5\\28437234-f1be-440d-9452-c51de1bd38ab\\scratchpad\\hacker-pdf\\text.txt","content":"PAGES 24\n\n===== PAGE 1 =====\nBioHacks \nMcMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario \nBioHacks 2026 \nHacker Package \nNovember 7-8, 2026 \n===== PAGE 2 =====\nWelcome to\nBioHacks\n2026 \nYou made it. For the next two days you and your\nteam will take on one patient case, choose a track\nand build something for him.\nThis package holds the schedule, the prizes, how to\nsubmit and the full case file. Read the case first.\nThen go build. \nDiscord \nEvery announcement this weekend goes out on the BioHacks Discord.\ndiscord.com/invite/mGZRP7vpj \nInstagram \nFollow \n@_biohacks \nand tag us in your posts from the weekend. \nOrganizers \nFind us at the check-in desk on the 2nd floor, outside the MCCD 2100 auditorium, or\nemail bioeng4youth@gmail.com. \nBioHacks 2026   2 \n===== PAGE 3 =====\nSafety and help \nMcMaster Security, 24 hours \n905-522-4135 \nFrom any campus phone, dial   88 EFRT , McMaster's Emergency First Response Team, is a\nteam of student first responders on campus. Reach them\nthrough Security at 905-522-4135 or 88. \nEmergency   911 \nAfter you call, tell the nearest organizer. \nCode of conduct \nBioHacks follows the MLH Code of\nConduct. Harassment of any kind is not\ntolerated. Report a concern to any\norganizer, or to MLH at +1 343 453 4532\nor incidents@mlh.io. \nFire alarm \nIf the alarm sounds, leave by the\nnearest exit and wait outside until an\norganizer says it is safe to return. \nOvernight \nHacking runs through the night. The\nMCCD 2100 auditorium is open for\nsleeping. Keep your laptop and\nvaluables with you or with a\nteammate. \nHalal and vegetarian options are served at every meal. \nBioHacks 2026   3 \n===== PAGE 4 =====\nSaturday, November 7 \nCeremonies   Guest lectures   Workshops   Food   Judging\nCheck-in, booths and deadline \nSchedule \n8 AM\n9 AM\n10 AM\n11 AM\n12 PM\n1 PM\n2 PM\n3 PM\n4 PM\n5 PM\n6 PM\n7 PM\n8 PM\n9 PM\n10 PM\n11 PM\n12 AM \nDoors open and check-in \n2nd floor, outside the MCCD 2100 auditorium \nOpening ceremony \nMCCD 2100 auditorium \nTeam formation   Team registration closes at 12:00 PM \nAnyone without a team is matched with one here. \nLunch \nArts Quad \nCAD for Medical Devices, from Sketch to 3D Print \nWorkshop, MCCD 3200 \nHacking with GitHub Copilot   MLH workshop, MCCD 7150 \nIntro to Google AI Studio   MLH workshop, MCCD 7150 \nGuest lecture with Dr. Tandon \nGUS Medical and Veterinary Schools, MCCD 3200 \nGuest lecture with Dr. Rock \nUHN Michener Institute of Education, MCCD 7140 \nDinner   Arts Quad \nTechTogether Meetup \nMLH, MCCD 7140 \nPitching workshop \nMCCD 2100 auditorium \nMidnight snack   MCCD 2100 auditorium \nTeam form \nSponsor booths \n11:00 AM to 6:30 PM\n2nd floor, outside the\nauditorium \nBioHacks 2026   4 \n===== PAGE 5 =====\nSunday, November 8 \nCeremonies   Guest lectures   Workshops   Food   Judging\nCheck-in, booths and deadline \nSchedule \n8 AM\n9 AM\n10 AM\n11 AM\n12 PM\n1 PM\n2 PM\n3 PM\n4 PM\n5 PM\n6 PM \nBreakfast \nMCCD 2100 auditorium \nSubmissions due on Devpost at 11:30 AM\nLunch \nArts Quad \nExpo judging \nMCCD 7110 and 7120 \nClosing ceremony \nMCCD 2100 auditorium \nEvent ends \nBioHacks 2026   5 \n===== PAGE 6 =====\nSubmission and\njudging \nProjects are due on Devpost\nby 11:30 AM on Sunday\n1   Team up.   Anyone without a team is matched with one during\nteam formation at 11:00 AM on Saturday. Form a team of 2 to 4\nand lock in one track on the team form by 12:00 PM on\nSaturday. Each team competes in exactly that one track and\nis judged only in it. \n2   Build.   Workshops, guest lectures, the MLH hardware\nlab and our 3D printers are open to every team. Ask\nan organizer to print a part. \n3   Submit.   One project per team on Devpost by\n11:30 AM on Sunday at \nbiohacksmcmaster.devpost.com .\nExternal sponsor prizes are optional. A team\ncan opt in to any of them on Devpost and win\nthem alongside its track result.   Devpost \n4   Present.   Expo judging runs from 1:30 to 3:00 PM on\nSunday in MCCD 7110 and 7120. Judges come to\nyour table. \n5   Celebrate.   Winners are announced at the closing ceremony\nat 4:30 PM in the MCCD 2100 auditorium. \nBioHacks 2026   6 \n===== PAGE 7 =====\nAlex Chen \nAlex is 47. He has taught senior biology at a high school on the Hamilton mountain for\nnineteen years, writes everything on the whiteboard and walks the rows while students\nwork. He coached cross-country for a decade and ran half-marathons until the spring of\n2025.\nHe lives in Westdale with his partner Jordan, a medical radiation technologist on 7 am to 4\npm shifts, and their two children, Maya, 16, and Leo, 14. In June 2024 a student asked\nwhether his marker had run dry. His handwriting had started to fade out halfway across the\nboard. The marker was fine. He waited a few months, then saw his family doctor. What\nfollows is his file. \nMountain Brow Family Health Team \nHamilton, Ontario\nReferral\n14 October 2024\nAlex Chen, 45, male\nTo: Plastic and Hand Surgery Clinic \nRe: Right hand weakness and deteriorating handwriting, four months \nThank you for seeing Alex, a 45-year-old right-handed teacher who is otherwise well. Since June he has\nnoticed that his handwriting fades across the page and that his right thumb and index finger tire quickly\nwhen writing on a whiteboard. He has started dropping his whiteboard marker and his coffee cup. No pain.\nNo numbness or tingling. He reports some cramping in the right forearm at night.\nOn examination the right thenar eminence looks a little flatter than the left. Grip is slightly weaker on the\nright. Phalen's test was equivocal. Sensation to light touch was normal in all fingers. He works at a desk in the\nevenings marking, and I suspect a carpal tunnel syndrome or a median neuropathy at the wrist.\nI would be grateful for your assessment and for nerve conduction studies at the wrist. He has no significant\npast history, takes no medication, does not smoke and runs regularly. \nDr. Priya Raman, MD, CCFP \nFamily physician \nPlastic and Hand Surgery Clinic \nConsultation and follow-up. Dictated, not read.\nConsultation 9 December 2024\nFollow-up 21 April 2025\nAlex Chen\nCopy to: Dr. P. Raman \nRe: Query right carpal tunnel syndrome \nConsultation.   Six months of right hand weakness affecting fine tasks, no sensory symptoms. Nerve\nconduction across the right wrist: median sensory and motor latencies within normal limits; no slowing\nacross the carpal tunnel. Visible wasting of the right thenar muscles and the muscle between thumb and\nindex finger. Grip 4 out of 5 on the right. Given the thenar wasting, his occupational demands and his wish to\ntry something, I offered a right carpal tunnel release as a reasonable trial of treatment. He elected to\nproceed after discussion of the risks and the uncertain benefit. Surgery 20 January 2025, uncomplicated. \nFollow-up, three months after surgery.   Wound well healed. He reports no improvement. If anything the\nhand is weaker, and he now notices twitching under the skin of the forearm, which he describes as \"rippling\".\nHe has also begun to mention his right foot catching on stairs. I have no further surgical options to offer.\nReturned to the care of his family physician with a suggestion that a neurological opinion may be helpful if\nsymptoms progress. \nDr. Marcus Oyelaran, MD, FRCSC \nPlastic and reconstructive surgery \nBioHacks 2026   7 \n===== PAGE 8 =====\nWestdale Physiotherapy and Sports Clinic \nDischarge summary\n10 February 2026\nAlex Chen\n12 August 2025 to 5 February 2026, 18 sessions\nTo: Dr. P. Raman \nRe: Right foot drop, treated as peroneal weakness. Not improving. \nAlex was referred in August 2025 for a right foot that catches on curbs and slaps on landing. He had\nstopped running in May because the right leg \"felt heavy and would not do what I asked\". A lumbar\nspine MRI in December 2025 was reported as unremarkable for his age, with no nerve root compression.\nOver six months of strengthening, balance work and a trial of an off-the-shelf ankle brace, his right\nankle dorsiflexion has gone from 4 out of 5 to 3 out of 5. Calf and thigh circumference on the right are\neach about 2 cm smaller than the left. He now uses a cane outside the house. He reports two falls, both\nin the evening, one on the stairs at home and one leaving the school at dusk. He has stopped cycling to\nwork and drives instead.\nThree things concern me. The weakness is progressing despite treatment. His reflexes are brisk,\nincluding at the right ankle, which is not what I would expect from a peripheral nerve problem. And he\nmentions that his right hand has been weak for well over a year and that a wrist operation did not help\nit. I do not think this is an ankle problem. I would strongly recommend a neurology referral, and I have\ntold Alex so. \nDaniela Ferreira, PT, MScPT \nRegistered physiotherapist \nMountain Brow Family Health Team \nHamilton, Ontario\nReferral, marked urgent\n16 February 2026\nAlex Chen, 46, male\nTo: Neurology, outpatient clinic \nRe: Progressive painless weakness, right arm and leg, twenty months \nI would be grateful if you could see Alex soon. I first saw him in October 2024 with right hand weakness\nthat I referred as a carpal tunnel syndrome. His wrist studies were normal, a carpal tunnel release in\nJanuary 2025 did not help, and in the past year he has developed a right foot drop that physiotherapy\nhas not improved and that a lumbar MRI does not explain.\nToday he reports muscle cramps, visible twitching in both forearms and lately the right thigh, and that\nhe has lost about 6 kg without trying since the summer. His voice is unchanged as far as he is aware,\nalthough his partner says he is quieter in the evenings. There is no pain, no numbness, no change in\nbladder or bowel function and no visual symptoms. On examination he has wasting of the right hand\nand brisk reflexes in all four limbs. I am concerned about a progressive neurological condition and\nwould value your opinion. I am sorry this has taken as long as it has. \nDr. Priya Raman, MD, CCFP \nBioHacks 2026   8 \n===== PAGE 9 =====\nNeurology Outpatient Clinic \nConsultation note. Neuromuscular service.\nSeen 11 June 2026\nReferral received 18 February 2026\nAlex Chen, 47, male\nReferring: Dr. P. Raman \nReason for referral: progressive painless weakness, right arm and leg, now two years\nHistory \nRight-handed, 47. First symptom June 2024: handwriting fading on the whiteboard, fatigue in the right\nthumb and index finger. Right carpal tunnel release January 2025 after normal wrist studies, no benefit.\nRight foot drop from spring 2025, progressing through physiotherapy; lumbar MRI unremarkable.\nCramps in both calves and the right forearm, worse at night. Fasciculations noticed in the forearms\nsince April 2025, now also in the right thigh. Unintentional weight loss, 86 kg two years ago to 78 kg\ntoday.\nOn direct questioning: speech is clear in the morning but his partner and students notice slurring by late\nafternoon, which he had put down to tiredness. Occasional coughing when drinking water quickly. He\nhas started sleeping on two pillows because he feels \"short of air\" lying flat. No diplopia, no sensory\nsymptoms, no sphincter change. Mood is low but he denies hopelessness. He is still teaching full time\nand still drives.\nFamily history: no neurological disease in parents, siblings or grandparents. No dementia. Social: lives\nwith partner and two teenagers, non-smoker, two drinks a week. No toxin exposure. \nExamination \nGeneral.   Thin. Weight 78 kg. Normal cognition in conversation, but on a bedside screen he produced\nonly nine words beginning with the same letter in one minute, with intact memory and orientation. \nCranial nerves.   Eye movements full. Face symmetric. Tongue shows mild wasting with fasciculations at\nrest. Jaw jerk brisk. Palate elevates symmetrically. Speech mildly spastic in quality late in the\nconsultation, intelligible. \nUpper limbs.   Marked wasting of the right thenar eminence and first dorsal interosseous, with relative\nsparing of the hypothenar muscles. Fasciculations in both forearms and the right deltoid. Tone\nincreased on the right. Power: right grip 4/5, right finger abduction 3/5, right thumb abduction 3/5, left\nupper limb 5/5. Reflexes brisk and symmetric, right more than left. Hoffmann sign present on the right. \nLower limbs.   Right calf wasting. Fasciculations in the right quadriceps. Tone increased bilaterally. Power:\nright ankle dorsiflexion 3/5, right hip flexion 4/5, left lower limb 5/5. Reflexes brisk, with three beats of\nclonus at the right ankle. Right plantar response extensor, left flexor. \nSensation.   Normal to light touch, pinprick, vibration and proprioception throughout.   Gait.   Right foot\ndrop with a high-stepping gait and a cane. Unable to walk on heels on the right. Romberg negative. \nInvestigations requested \nNerve conduction studies and needle electromyography, four regions. MRI brain and cervical spine.\nBloods as per neuromuscular panel. Pulmonary function tests upright and supine. Referral to speech-\nlanguage pathology. Review in four weeks with results. \nDr. Hannah Weiss-Okafor, MD, FRCPC \nNeurologist, neuromuscular disease \nBioHacks 2026   9 \n===== PAGE 10 =====\nElectromyography Laboratory \nNerve conduction studies and needle EMG\n18 June 2026\nAlex Chen\nRequested by: Dr. H. Weiss-Okafor \nNerve conduction studies \nNerve   Result   Reference \nRight median motor, to thumb\nmuscles \nAmplitude 1.6 mV, latency and velocity\nnormal\nAmplitude above 4.0\nmV \nRight ulnar motor, to little-finger\nmuscles \nAmplitude 5.5 mV, velocity normal, no\nconduction block\nAmplitude above 6.0\nmV \nRight peroneal motor, to foot   Amplitude 0.9 mV, velocity normal   Amplitude above 2.0\nmV \nLeft median and ulnar motor   Amplitudes at lower limit of normal \nSensory studies, median, ulnar,\nsural, both sides \nAll normal   Normal \nNeedle electromyography \nRegion   Muscles sampled   Findings \nBulbar   Tongue   Fasciculation potentials, fibrillations, enlarged\nmotor units \nCervical   Right first dorsal interosseous,\nright biceps, left deltoid\nFibrillations and positive sharp waves,\nfasciculations, large polyphasic motor units with\nreduced recruitment, right more than left \nThoracic   Right T9 paraspinal   Fibrillations, fasciculations \nLumbosacral   Right tibialis anterior, right vastus\nlateralis, left gastrocnemius\nActive denervation and chronic reinnervation in\nall three; severely reduced recruitment in tibialis\nanterior \nSummary \nWidespread active denervation and chronic reinnervation in four body regions, including clinically\nunaffected muscles, with normal sensory conduction and no conduction block. There is no\nelectrophysiological evidence of a peripheral neuropathy, a disorder of the neuromuscular junction, or\na myopathy. \nDr. Hannah Weiss-Okafor, MD, FRCPC \nBioHacks 2026   10 \n===== PAGE 11 =====\nLaboratory Results \nNeuromuscular panel, venous blood\nCollected 12 June 2026\nAlex Chen \nTest   Result   Reference \nCreatine kinase   470 U/L   Below 195 U/L \nComplete blood count, electrolytes, renal and\nliver function, glucose, calcium, thyroid,\nvitamin B12 \nAll within reference range \nSerum protein electrophoresis and\nimmunofixation \nNo paraprotein   None \nHIV, syphilis, Lyme serology; anti-GM1\nantibodies; heavy metals \nNegative   Negative \nSerum neurofilament light chain   88 pg/mL   Below 15 pg/mL at\nage 47 \nGenetic panel: C9orf72 repeat expansion, SOD1,\nTARDBP, FUS and 28 further genes \nNo pathogenic variant detected   Reported 30 July\n2026 \nPulmonary Function Laboratory \nSpirometry, upright and supine\n18 June 2026\nAlex Chen \nMeasure   Result   Reference \nForced vital capacity, sitting   78 per cent of predicted   Above 80 per cent \nForced vital capacity, lying flat   64 per cent of predicted   Within 10 per cent of\nthe sitting value \nSniff nasal inspiratory pressure   52 cm H 2 O   Above 70 cm H 2 O \nComment: the fall in vital capacity on lying flat indicates diaphragm weakness. \nBioHacks 2026   11 \n===== PAGE 12 =====\nDiagnostic Imaging \nMRI brain and cervical spine, with and without contrast\n16 June 2026\nAlex Chen\nRequested by: Dr. H. Weiss-Okafor \nClinical information: progressive upper and lower limb weakness with brisk reflexes. Exclude cord\ncompression. Query motor neuron disease. \nA. Brain, axial FLAIR   at the level of\nthe internal capsule. Symmetric\nhyperintensity along the posterior\nlimb of the internal capsule on\nboth sides (arrows), following the\ncorticospinal tracts. \nB. Brain, axial susceptibility-\nweighted   near the vertex. A thin\nband of low signal along the\nprecentral gyrus on both sides\n(arrows), sometimes called the\nmotor band sign. \nC. Cervical spine, sagittal T2. \nNormal alignment. No disc\nprotrusion, canal stenosis, cord\ncompression or intrinsic cord\nsignal change from the foramen\nmagnum to T2. \nImpression \n- Bilateral, symmetric corticospinal tract signal change and a motor band sign. These findings are\nsupportive of a degenerative process affecting the upper motor neurons in the appropriate clinical\ncontext. They are not specific.\n- No structural lesion of the brain or cervical cord to account for the patient's weakness. Specifically, no\ncervical spondylotic myelopathy, no demyelinating lesion, no mass.\n- No abnormal enhancement after contrast, so nothing to suggest tumour, infection or inflammation. \nDr. Thanh Nguyen, MD, FRCPC \nNeuroradiology \nBioHacks 2026   12 \n===== PAGE 13 =====\nNeurology Outpatient Clinic \nLetter to referring physician. Copy given to the patient at his request.\n14 July 2026\nAlex Chen, 47, male\nTo: Dr. P. Raman \nDiagnosis: amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), limb onset, with early bulbar and respiratory\ninvolvement \nDear Dr. Raman,\nI saw Alex and his partner Jordan today to go through his results, and I am sorry to say that the\ndiagnosis is ALS. I spent the appointment with them and the clinic social worker, and they will return in\ntwo weeks to talk further. He asked that this letter be written in plain language and that he be given a\ncopy, which I have done. \nHow the diagnosis was made.   ALS is diagnosed on the clinical picture, not on any single test. Alex has\nprogressive weakness with signs of both upper motor neuron damage (brisk reflexes, clonus, a\nHoffmann sign, an upgoing plantar, spastic speech) and lower motor neuron damage (wasting,\nfasciculations, low motor amplitudes, denervation on EMG) in the same regions, now spread across all\nfour: bulbar, cervical, thoracic and lumbosacral. Sensation is normal. The MRI excludes the structural\nconditions that can mimic this, the bloods exclude the treatable ones, and the EMG shows the process\nis active and widespread. He meets the Gold Coast criteria without ambiguity. In retrospect he probably\nmet them a year ago. \nWhat ALS is.   A progressive loss of the motor neurons that drive voluntary muscle, in the brain and the\nspinal cord. The muscle itself is healthy; it wastes because the nerve supply dies. Sensation, eye\nmovements, bladder and bowel are spared. Thinking is usually spared, though about half of patients\ndevelop some change in word-finding or executive function, and Alex's reduced verbal fluency is of that\nkind. The cause in his case, as in about nine of ten cases, is unknown. \nTreatment.   There is no cure. Two drugs have been shown to slow progression modestly: riluzole, which I\nhave started today, and edaravone, which we will discuss. A third drug, tofersen, is the first treatment to\nact on a cause of ALS. It is an antisense oligonucleotide that silences a faulty SOD1 gene, and because it\ncannot cross the blood-brain barrier it is given by lumbar puncture every month. Health Canada\nauthorised it with conditions in February 2025. It applies to the roughly two per cent of patients who\ncarry a SOD1 mutation. Alex's genetic panel was negative, so, like most people with this disease, he has\nno treatment directed at its cause. Beyond drugs, what has been shown to lengthen life is care at a\nmultidisciplinary clinic, keeping his weight up, and breathing support at night once it is needed. \nWhat to expect.   His progression over two years has been steady rather than rapid, which is in his\nfavour. The pattern will continue: the right hand and leg will weaken further and the left side will follow;\nspeech, swallowing and breathing have begun to be involved and will progress. I have been honest with\nthem that the median survival from symptom onset is two to four years, that the range is wide, and that\nthe decisions ahead (a feeding tube, ventilation at night, how he wants to communicate when speech\nis difficult) are better made early. I have referred him to the regional ALS clinic, which will see him every\nthree months, and to ALS Canada for equipment and support.\nHe intends to keep teaching this year. I see no reason he should not. \nDr. Hannah Weiss-Okafor, MD, FRCPC \nNeurologist, neuromuscular disease \nBioHacks 2026   13 \n===== PAGE 14 =====\nRegional ALS Clinic \nMultidisciplinary summary. First visit.\n22 September 2026\nAlex Chen, 47\n27 months from first symptom, 10 weeks from diagnosis \nMedication   Riluzole 50 mg twice daily since July. Tolerating. Edaravone declined for now.\nLiving situation   Two-storey house, bedroom and only full bathroom upstairs, fourteen stairs with a rail\non the left. Partner works 7 am to 4 pm; Alex is alone at home on his non-teaching\ndays and from the end of school until 4:30 pm. Children are at school until 3 pm.\nWork   Full-time teaching, four classes a day. School has agreed to a stool and a microphone\non request; he has not asked for either. \nNeurology \nALSFRS-R 32 of 48 (bulbar 9, fine motor 6, gross motor 7, breathing 10). Decline of about 0.6 points per\nmonth since onset, roughly linear. Right hand now 3/5 grip, cannot sustain a pinch for more than a few\nseconds. Left hand weaker than in June: grip 4/5, early first dorsal interosseous wasting. Cognitive\nscreen (ECAS) 101 of 136, with verbal fluency and executive scores below cut-off and memory, language\nand visuospatial scores normal. \nSpeech-language pathology \nSpeech is intelligible but effortful by late afternoon. Reading passage: 165 words per minute at 9 am, 118\nat 4 pm; sentence intelligibility 98 per cent in the morning, 91 per cent in the afternoon. He reports that\nstudents at the back of the room have started asking him to repeat himself after lunch and that he has\nstopped doing the end-of-day recap. Voice banking discussed; he has not started and is reluctant (\"it\nfeels like giving up\"). Timed water swallow: cough on thin liquid; solids and thickened liquids unaffected.\nHe has started avoiding eating in the staff room. \nDietitian \nWeight 77.5 kg, from 86 kg. Meals take 35 to 40 minutes; he tires before finishing. Right-handed cutlery\nno longer practical; he has been eating with a spoon in the left hand. Calorie supplement started.\nFeeding tube discussed as a future decision while breathing capacity is still above 50 per cent. He says\nSunday cooking for the family is the thing he least wants to give up. \nOccupational therapy \nHandwriting now illegible after about ten minutes. Marks by typing with the left hand and two right\nfingers; phone use is one-handed and slow. Buttons, zips and shoelaces take help in the morning. Drives\nan automatic with the left foot off the pedals, which he has not told his insurer. Stairs are the main\nhazard: both falls were after 6 pm on the stairs or at dusk. Advised against carrying anything on the\nstairs. Home assessment booked. He has declined a stairlift \"for now\". \nPhysiotherapy and respiratory \n10-metre walk with cane 14.2 seconds. Ankle-foot orthosis fitted on the right, which he wears to school\nbut not at home. Forced vital capacity 76 per cent sitting, 61 per cent supine. Sleeps on two pillows,\nwakes once or twice with a dry mouth. Non-invasive ventilation discussed; not yet indicated. Cough\npeak flow 310 L/min. \nSocial work \nHas disclosed to the school and to the children. Finances adequate while he works; partner's shifts\ncannot easily change. Wants to be \"reachable and useful\" while alone in the afternoons. Would like to\nget to his daughter's graduation in two years without a wheelchair. \nBioHacks 2026   14 \n===== PAGE 15 =====\nMeasurements \nMeasure   Alex   Typical\nHand \nGrip strength, dynamometer   Right 14 kg, left 32 kg   About 48 kg \nKey pinch   Right 3 kg, left 8 kg   About 11 kg \nNine-hole peg test   Right 44 s, left 23 s   About 20 s \nSpeech and swallowing \nSpeaking rate, same passage, 9 am and 4\npm \n165 and 118 words per minute   150 to 190 \nSentence intelligibility, 9 am and 4 pm   98 and 91 per cent   Above 97 per cent \nTimed water swallow, 100 mL   Cough on thin liquid   No cough \nBreathing \nForced vital capacity, sitting and lying flat   76 and 61 per cent of\npredicted\nAbove 80; within 10 per\ncent of sitting \nWalking \n10-metre walk with cane   14.2 s   Under 8 s \nFalls in the past three months   Two, both after 6 pm   None \nProgression \nALSFRS-R at 0, 6, 12, 18, 24 and 27 months   48, 43, 39, 36, 33, 32   48 \nSerum neurofilament light chain   88 pg/mL   Below 15 pg/mL at his age \nWeight   77.5 kg; 86 kg two years ago \nBioHacks 2026   15 \n===== PAGE 16 =====\nFrom Alex \nYou have just read two years of my life in about twenty minutes.\nI am still me. I still get up at six and I still argue about the Leafs. This thing\nhas taken my right hand and some of my voice, and it is going to take\nmore. The people around me are still here. My wife still laughs at my\njokes. My kids still climb on me. My students still email me at midnight.\nI want to be in a classroom in June. I want to be the one who cooks on\nSunday, even if it takes me all afternoon. I want my kids to remember me\nas their dad and not as a patient.\nWhatever you build this weekend, I hope it comes from a place of caring\nabout someone like me. If it helps one person hold on to one of those\nordinary things a little longer, you will have done something that matters\nto a whole family. Thank you for spending your weekend on us. \nAlex \nBioHacks 2026   16 \n===== PAGE 17 =====\nThe tracks \nChoose one track to focus on. \n1. Detection and Diagnostics \nAlex waited two years for a diagnosis, through four clinicians and an\noperation that did nothing.\nIn Canada the wait is typically 15 to 27 months depending on the province.\nSince diagnosis his disease is followed with a questionnaire every three\nmonths.\nBuild something that detects the disease earlier or predicts how it will\nprogress. Use the datasets on the next page or find your own. \n2. Therapeutics and Delivery \nThe one drug that acts on a cause of ALS applies to about two in a\nhundred patients. Alex is not one of them.\nThe two other approved drugs add months. More than fifty late-stage trials\nhave failed since riluzole was approved in 1995.\nGetting anything into a spinal motor neuron is itself an unsolved problem.\nPropose a therapy, or a way of delivering one, that could slow the disease\nor protect the neurons that remain. Say what it would mean for Alex now\nand for someone where he was in 2024. \n3. Wearable Devices and Quality of Life \nHe is strong in the morning and weak by the afternoon. He teaches in a\nclassroom where he needs to be heard, he is alone at home until 4:30, and\nhe has fallen on the stairs.\nHis right hand has a few seconds of pinch left. His left is starting to go.\nMost assistive devices end up in a drawer because nobody asked the\nperson what they wanted.\nDesign a device or tool that lets Alex keep one thing that matters to him as\nhis abilities change. The MLH hardware lab is open all weekend if you want\nto build. \nBioHacks 2026   17 \n===== PAGE 18 =====\nData \nDataset   What it is   Access \nALS Focus, Harvard\nDataverse \nSurvey data from the ALS Association: self-reported\nALSFRS-R and vital capacity over time (892 records),\ndemographics for 1,545 people with ALS and\ncaregivers, health status and telehealth responses,\nwith a data dictionary.\nOpen download after\naccepting the data use\nterms. No account. \nALS progression data,\nEfron and Hastie \n1,822 people with ALS from the 2012 DREAM challenge,\ndrawn from clinical trial records: demographics, onset\nsite, symptoms, ALSFRS items, vital capacity, vitals and\nweight summarised per patient, with the rate of\nALSFRS decline (dFRS) as the target. 371 columns.\nDocumented in the CASIdata R package.\nOpen download, one text\nfile. No account. \nGait in\nNeurodegenerative\nDisease, PhysioNet \nFoot-force and stride-interval recordings from 13\npeople with ALS, 15 with Parkinson's, 20 with\nHuntington's and 16 controls.\nOpen download, no\naccount. \nVOC-ALS, Synapse   1,224 recordings of sustained vowels and syllable\nrepetition from 102 people with ALS and 51 matched\ncontrols, labelled with ALSFRS-R speech score and\ndysarthria severity. Paper: Scientific Data 2024.\nFree Synapse account\n(registered user).\nSynapse terms of use. \nbigP3BCI, PhysioNet   P300 speller EEG from 326 participant sessions across\n20 studies, including people with ALS with ALSFRS-R in\nthe file headers. 10 GB zipped; single studies can be\npulled separately.\nOpen download. CC BY\n4.0. \nEEGET-ALS, Figshare   32-channel EEG and eye tracking from six people with\nALS using an eye-gaze speller at home over several\nmonths, and 170 controls. 1.6 GB. Paper: Scientific Data\n2024.\nOpen download, no\naccount. CC0. \nSeattle ALS Patient\nProfile Database \nInterviews every three months for 18 months with 143\npeople with ALS and 123 partners: coping, support,\ndevices, psychological health, outcomes. Mendeley\nData.\nOpen download. CC BY-\nNC 3.0. \nBioHacks 2026   18 \n===== PAGE 19 =====\nReferences \n1. ALS Society of Canada. ReferALS early referral tool for physicians and patients.\nals.ca/resource/referals-early-referral-tool\n2. Shefner JM, Al-Chalabi A, Baker MR, et al. A proposal for new diagnostic criteria for ALS (the Gold\nCoast criteria). Clin Neurophysiol. 2020;131(8):1975-1978.\n3. Richards D, Morren JA, Pioro EP. Time to diagnosis and factors affecting diagnostic delay in\namyotrophic lateral sclerosis. In: Araki T, ed. Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis. Exon Publications; 2021.\n4. Hodgkinson VL, et al. Provincial differences in the diagnosis and care of amyotrophic lateral\nsclerosis. Can J Neurol Sci. 2018;45(6):652-659.\n5. Westeneng HJ, et al. Prognosis for patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: development and\nvalidation of a personalised prediction model. Lancet Neurol. 2018;17(5):423-433.\n6. Health Canada. Summary Basis of Decision, Qalsody (tofersen). Notice of Compliance with\nconditions, 28 February 2025.\n7. Phillips B, Zhao H. Predictors of assistive technology abandonment. Assist Technol. 1993;5(1):36-45.\n8. Ontario Ministry of Health. Assistive Devices Program. ontario.ca/page/assistive-devices-program \nA note on the images \nThe MRI images on page 12 are reproduced from open-access teaching material. They illustrate the\nfindings described in the report and are not from one patient. Alex, his family and every clinician and\npractice in this file are fictional. \nBioHacks 2026   19 \n===== PAGE 20 =====\nJudging rubric \nDetection and Diagnostics \nCriterion   Level 4   Level 3   Level 2   Level 1\nClinical\ngrounding \n20 \n20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \nDiagnostic\nvalue \n30 \n30 points   22 points   15 points   8 points \nEvidence and\ntesting \n20 \n20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \nFeasibility \n15 \n15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \nCommunication \n15 \n15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \nClear connection\nto the patient's\nfindings, abilities\nand priorities\nDesign choices\nfollow from the\ncase\nGood connection\nto the case\nSome design\nchoices not tied\nto it\nLoose connection\nto the case\nCould apply to\nany patient with\nthis condition\nLittle or no\nconnection to the\ncase\nClear explanation\nof what is\ndetected and why\nit matters\nClear advantage\nover current\npractice\nGood explanation\nof what is\ndetected\nAdvantage over\ncurrent practice\nstated with minor\ngaps\nAdequate\nexplanation of\nwhat is detected\nAdvantage over\ncurrent practice\nunclear\nUnclear what is\ndetected or why it\nmatters\nStrong reasoning\nfor why it works,\nsupported by\ndata,\nmeasurements or\na prototype\nClear plan for how\nthe method would\nbe tested and\naccuracy\nmeasured\nGood reasoning\nwith some\nsupporting\nevidence\nGood testing plan\nwith minor gaps\nReasoning\npresent but little\nevidence\nTesting\nmentioned but\nnot developed\nNo clear\nreasoning or\nevidence\nNo testing\nconsidered\nClear and realistic\npath to use, with\ncost, access and\nregulation\nconsidered\nGood\nconsideration of\npractical barriers\nwith minor gaps\nPractical barriers\nacknowledged\nbut not addressed\nPractical barriers\nmissing or\nignored\nClear, organised\nand concise\nQuestions\nanswered well\nClear overall with\nminor issues\nMost questions\nanswered\nHard to follow in\nplaces\nQuestions partly\nanswered\nUnclear or\ndisorganised\nQuestions not\nanswered \nJudges score each project out of 100 with this rubric, then name the best, second\nbest and third best project they saw in the track. The rankings from all judges\ndetermine which two teams win in each track. \nBioHacks 2026   20 \n===== PAGE 21 =====\nJudging rubric \nTherapeutics and Delivery \nCriterion   Level 4   Level 3   Level 2   Level 1\nClinical\ngrounding \n20 \n20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \nMechanism\nand rationale \n30 \n30 points   22 points   15 points   8 points \nReaching the\npatient \n20 \n20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \nFeasibility \n15 \n15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \nCommunication \n15 \n15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \nClear connection\nto the patient's\nfindings, abilities\nand priorities\nDesign choices\nfollow from the\ncase\nGood connection\nto the case\nSome design\nchoices not tied\nto it\nLoose connection\nto the case\nCould apply to\nany patient with\nthis condition\nLittle or no\nconnection to the\ncase\nClear and well\nsupported\nexplanation of\nhow the\ntreatment acts on\nthe condition\nScientifically\nsound\nGood explanation\nof the mechanism\nwith minor gaps\nor light support\nMechanism\nstated but weakly\nexplained or\nsupported\nMechanism\nunclear or\nmissing\nClear explanation\nof how the\ntreatment\nreaches where it\nacts\nMain risks\nidentified and\naddressed\nGood explanation\nof delivery\nRisks identified\nwith minor gaps\nDelivery or risks\nmentioned but\nnot developed\nDelivery and risks\nnot considered\nClear and realistic\npath to use, with\ncost, access and\nregulation\nconsidered\nGood\nconsideration of\npractical barriers\nwith minor gaps\nPractical barriers\nacknowledged\nbut not addressed\nPractical barriers\nmissing or\nignored\nClear, organised\nand concise\nQuestions\nanswered well\nClear overall with\nminor issues\nMost questions\nanswered\nHard to follow in\nplaces\nQuestions partly\nanswered\nUnclear or\ndisorganised\nQuestions not\nanswered \nJudges score each project out of 100 with this rubric, then name the best, second\nbest and third best project they saw in the track. The rankings from all judges\ndetermine which two teams win in each track. \nBioHacks 2026   21 \n===== PAGE 22 =====\nJudging rubric \nWearable Devices and Quality of Life \nCriterion   Level 4   Level 3   Level 2   Level 1\nClinical\ngrounding \n20 \n20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \nDesign and\nfunction \n30 \n30 points   22 points   15 points   8 points \nUsability in\ndaily life \n20 \n20 points   15 points   10 points   5 points \nFeasibility \n15 \n15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \nCommunication \n15 \n15 points   11 points   8 points   4 points \nClear connection\nto the patient's\nfindings, abilities\nand priorities\nDesign choices\nfollow from the\ncase\nGood connection\nto the case\nSome design\nchoices not tied\nto it\nLoose connection\nto the case\nCould apply to\nany patient with\nthis condition\nLittle or no\nconnection to the\ncase\nClear and\ncomplete design\nthat does what it\nclaims\nSupported by a\nprototype, model\nor demonstration\nGood design with\nminor gaps\nLimited\ndemonstration\nPartial design or\nunclear function\nUnclear what the\ndevice does\nComfortable and\nlow effort for the\npatient\nAdapts as the\ncondition\nprogresses\nUsable day to day\nAdapts to some\nprogression of the\ncondition\nUsable only in\nideal conditions\nImpractical for\ndaily use\nClear and realistic\npath to use, with\ncost, access and\nregulation\nconsidered\nGood\nconsideration of\npractical barriers\nwith minor gaps\nPractical barriers\nacknowledged\nbut not addressed\nPractical barriers\nmissing or\nignored\nClear, organised\nand concise\nQuestions\nanswered well\nClear overall with\nminor issues\nMost questions\nanswered\nHard to follow in\nplaces\nQuestions partly\nanswered\nUnclear or\ndisorganised\nQuestions not\nanswered \nJudges score each project out of 100 with this rubric, then name the best, second\nbest and third best project they saw in the track. The rankings from all judges\ndetermine which two teams win in each track. \nBioHacks 2026   22 \n===== PAGE 23 =====\nPrizes \nFirst place \n$1,000 in prizes for the top team overall \nEach track has two winning teams. Track winners receive cash\nprizes, with amounts announced on Sunday. \nExternal sponsor prizes \nThese prizes sit outside the three tracks. A team can win any of them alongside its track\nresult. Opt in on Devpost. \nBest 3D Print \nFilament \nBest Use of\nElevenLabs \nWireless\nearbuds \nBest Use of Gemini\nAPI \nMLH swag kits \nBest Use of Entire \nMini portable\nprojector \nBest Use of Solana \nUnruggable\nhardware\nwallets \nBest Use of Tiger\nData \nStream Deck\nMini \nBest Use of Vultr \nAssorted prizes \nBest Domain Name\nfrom GoDaddy\nRegistry \nDigital gift card \nBest vlog \n$100 Amazon gift\ncard \nVlog your weekend at BioHacks, post it on\nInstagram and tag @_biohacks, then drop\nthe link in the vlogs channel on the Discord.\nThe best vlog wins. \nWorkshop raffle \n1 year of NordVPN, NordPass and\nIncogni, plus Saily eSIM data \nAttend three of the four workshops and both\nguest lectures to enter. Six winners are drawn\nat the closing ceremony. \nBioHacks 2026   23 \n===== PAGE 24 =====\nHosts, partners and sponsors \nHosts \nBioHacks   iBioMed Society \nPartner\nHeadline sponsors \nSponsors \nMLH partners \nBioHacks 2026   24 ","numLines":1061,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1061}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"61a609bb-3bbc-4371-b026-8df611157651","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"session_id":"28437234-f1be-440d-9452-c51de1bd38ab","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\jerem\\BioHacks-2026\\frontend","sessionId":"28437234-f1be-440d-9452-c51de1bd38ab","version":"2.1.319","gitBranch":"frontend/diagnosis"}
