{"parentUuid":"3af637e8-8f5e-4353-8995-2e7ceba74287","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgpxrdevsARk6p8PxXomL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_read_input_tokens":857971,"output_tokens":1145,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":45},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1037,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1145,"cache_read_input_tokens":857971,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1037},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":7332,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"9e8ea44e-2925-475a-bb93-ec9eb2ee11e4","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgpxrdGsCT7umLZ1vK9iu","requestRef":"7042d30a-7071-4dac-94f0-7eaecd06ba81","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"696a1c3b-7e24-4eb4-ab28-52e7b4543fd3","timestamp":"2026-11-08T06:10:25.712Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","session_id":"28437234-f1be-440d-9452-c51de1bd38ab","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\jerem\\BioHacks-2026\\frontend","sessionId":"28437234-f1be-440d-9452-c51de1bd38ab","version":"2.1.319","gitBranch":"frontend/diagnosis"}
{"parentUuid":"696a1c3b-7e24-4eb4-ab28-52e7b4543fd3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgpxrdevsARk6p8PxXomL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The PDF really is parsed, but it reads Alex's file properly and most other PDFs poorly.\n\n**What happens on upload**\n1. The PDF's text is extracted in the browser.\n2. `parseCasePatient` (in `case-patient.ts`) searches that text for specific phrases and builds the patient profile.\n3. That profile replaces the sample patient on the patient card, in the analysis agent's trace, in the report header, and on the Runs page.\n\n**How it did on real PDFs:**\n\n| Field | Hacker package (Alex) | My test PDF (same facts, different wording) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Name | Alex Chen | Not found (\"Uploaded patient\") |\n| Age / sex | 47 / M | Not found |\n| Onset | Limb onset | Bulbar onset |\n| ALSFRS-R | 32 | 38 |\n| Slope | −0.6 pts/mo | Not found |\n| FVC | 76% (latest, correctly) | Not found |\n| Genetics | Panel negative (+ genes) | Not found |\n| Medication | Riluzole 50 mg twice daily | Not found |\n| Location | Hamilton, Ontario | Toronto, ON |\n| Preferences | 4 quotes from the file | None |\n\nOn Alex's file it's very good: it even takes the latest values when a fact changes over time (age 45 → 47, FVC 78 → 76). Its two tests pass.\n\nOn my test PDF it found 3 of 10 fields, although the facts were all there. The parser matches the hacker package's exact phrasing, so ordinary wording slips past it:\n\n| Field | My test PDF says | The parser expects |\n|---|---|---|\n| Age | \"58-year-old woman\" | \"Alex Chen, 47, male\" |\n| FVC | \"FVC 72% predicted\" | \"vital capacity … 78 per cent\" |\n| Slope | \"slope -0.9 points/month\" | \"decline of about 0.6 points per month\" |\n| Medication | \"Medications: riluzole…\" | \"Medication Riluzole…\" (singular, capital letter) |\n| Genetics | \"Genetics: ALS panel negative\" | \"Genetic panel: … No pathogenic variant detected\" |\n\nIt never invents a value: anything it misses shows as \"Not stated\" or a dash, which is the right failure mode.\n\n**Two other limits:**\n- **Scanned PDFs:** these have no text layer, so nothing would be read at all. That would need OCR.\n- **Results after step 1 don't use the file:** the profiles, DRKG matches, candidate scores and report content are still scripted. Uploading a different patient changes the name and profile shown, not which drugs come out.\n\n**Options**, if other PDFs need to work:\n1. **Widen the patterns** for the common phrasings above, plus \"%\", \"y/o\", \"Meds:\" and the like. This is cheap, keeps everything in the browser and matches reliably, but it will always miss some wordings.\n2. **Have an AI model read the PDF** and fill in the profile fields. This copes with any wording, but needs an API key and a backend, and the patient text leaves the browser.\n\nFor the demo, option 1 plus uploading Alex's file is the safe choice. `case-patient.ts` is a teammate's uncommitted work, so I haven't changed it. Want me to widen the patterns?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_read_input_tokens":857971,"output_tokens":1145,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":45},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1037,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1145,"cache_read_input_tokens":857971,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1037},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"9e8ea44e-2925-475a-bb93-ec9eb2ee11e4","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgpxrdGsCT7umLZ1vK9